# BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding Notes

> July 27th, 2026

## Why I Read It

After reading the GPT paper, I wanted to understand the other major transformer architecture: **BERT**.

My goal was to understand why BERT became the standard for language understanding while GPT became the foundation for language generation. I also wanted to understand how encoder-only transformers differ from decoder-only transformers.

---

## Context

Before BERT, most language models (including GPT) trained by predicting text in a left-to-right manner.

This meant that every token could only use the words that came before it when learning.

Although this worked well for language generation, it limited how well the model could understand the complete context of a sentence.

Google proposed BERT to solve this problem by introducing **deep bidirectional representations**, allowing every token to attend to both the left and right context during pretraining.

---

## Core Idea

The core idea of BERT was to pretrain a **bidirectional Transformer encoder** on large amounts of unlabeled text before fine-tuning it for downstream NLP tasks.

Instead of predicting the next word like GPT, BERT learns by hiding random words and predicting the missing tokens using information from both directions.

This allows the model to build much richer contextual representations of language.

---

## Architecture

### Pretraining

* Multi-layer **Transformer Encoder**
* Bidirectional self-attention
* Masked Language Modeling (MLM)
* Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)

Unlike GPT, BERT does **not** use causal masking.

Every token is allowed to attend to every other token in the sequence during pretraining.

---

### Fine-tuning

After pretraining, the same encoder can be adapted to many NLP tasks by attaching a small task-specific output head.

Tasks include:

* Question Answering
* Named Entity Recognition
* Sentiment Analysis
* Natural Language Inference
* Sentence Classification

The underlying encoder remains the same while only the prediction head changes.

---

## Training Process

### Model

* Transformer Encoder
* BERT Base (12 layers)
* BERT Large (24 layers)

### Pretraining Objectives

**Masked Language Modeling (MLM)**

* Randomly mask tokens
* Predict the original masked words
* Forces the model to understand context from both directions

**Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)**

* Predict whether two sentences originally appeared consecutively
* Designed to help the model learn relationships between sentences

### Fine-tuning

* Small learning rates (typically between 2e-5 and 5e-5)
* Only a few epochs are needed
* Add a task-specific prediction head
* Fine-tune the entire model

---

## What Surprised Me

The authors repeatedly stated that GPT and BERT were very similar.

Initially this confused me because GPT is a decoder-only model while BERT is an encoder-only model.

After reading further, I realized they were referring to the **building blocks** of the architecture rather than how the models operate.

Both models use the Transformer architecture, but they differ mainly in the attention masking strategy and the learning objective.

Another surprise was how much emphasis the paper placed on **bidirectional attention**.

Reading the paper made me realize that understanding language and generating language are fundamentally different problems.

